  "discussions_data": [
    {
      "id": "2f97c3ef30178aae56a44ab4c72b0e83e15a947e",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2083170770,
          "type": null,
          "body": "> That means that if someone types a capture filter like \"host www.sonic.com\", pcap_compile will perform getaddrinfo calls for \"w\" \"ww\" \"www\" and so on.\n\n`pcap_compile()` from tcpdump.org doesn't do that; it just calls `getaddrinfo()` on the name in the filter expression.  The *resolver* presumably responds to getting a CNAME record by following the symlink :-), and, if that results in another CNAME record, you end up doing a lot of lookups, but that's not libpcap's doing.  (And www.sonic.com resolves to a CNAME of sonic.com, which, in turn, resolves to a bunch of records, at least according to nslookup.)\n\nIf `pcap_compile()` is called multiple times, as the filter name checking does, that's a different matter, but the multiple `getaddrinfo()` calls are due to those multiple `pcap_compile()` calls, not due to `pcap_compile()` making multiple calls for the same name.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-08-31T22:29:08.953Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-08-31T22:32:02.274Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 324341159,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 17072,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "fcdd86ada7601f4ab3c30dc62a0dd3b835e8df66",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2083262052,
          "type": null,
          "body": "Yes, to be specific, it prevents `pcap_compile()` from being called (and blocking the worker thread) every time the line edit is changed at all, only calling it after a sufficiently long pause in typing, set to the advanced GUI preference already used for that purpose elsewhere.",
